  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x=0\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y & =x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2{e}^{-x})}_{\u23df}}_{P(x)}y & ={{{e}^{-2x}x}_{\u23df}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2{e}^{-x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{-2x}x.$",
